DEL (0x7F) occupies a cell in Tera Term; vte discards it.
`charset.cpp:ParseFirstUTF8` sends every byte <= 0x7F that is not C0 to
PutU32, so DEL is stored as a cell holding U+007F -- but only in the ground
state: inside an escape sequence it is stripped, and `ESC [ <DEL> 1 m` is
still a valid SGR. Reproducing that needs to know which parser state we are
in, and `vte` does not expose it; ECMA-48, xterm, VTE and alacritty all
ignore DEL, so vte is arguably right and Tera Term is the outlier.
Costs one invisible cell of divergence. Revisit if the parser is ever forked.
